counterexample:
-- Under the given schema no counterexample exists. 
-- Any test data that obeys the constraints will cause both queries to produce the same result. 
-- For example, the following INSERT statements (which obey the schema) show equivalent behavior:

INSERT INTO EVENT (EVENT_ID, EVENT_NAME) VALUES ('E1', 'September Speaker');
INSERT INTO EVENT (EVENT_ID, EVENT_NAME) VALUES ('E2', 'Other Event');

INSERT INTO BUDGET (BUDGET_ID, AMOUNT, LINK_TO_EVENT) VALUES ('B1', 100, 'E1');
INSERT INTO BUDGET (BUDGET_ID, AMOUNT, LINK_TO_EVENT) VALUES ('B2', 50, 'E2');

-- Q1 joins on EVENT and filters for 'September Speaker': it sums only the 100 from budget row B1.
-- Q2 uses a subquery to get event IDs with name 'September Speaker' (which is only 'E1') and sums the 100 from B1.

-- Therefore, both queries yield the same result. Any attempt to create a counterexample (for instance, by having duplicate event rows) would violate the primary key constraint on EVENT.

